# Jenkins pipelines

Edit `Jenkinsfile` / pipeline entrypoints as **portable CI definitions**.
Match the repository’s existing declarative or scripted style. Do not invent
a second Pipeline dialect or hard-code a product’s private job graph.

Also follow `jenkins-ci-from-main` for SCM pickup reminders. For author /
review checklists, prefer skill `jenkins-pipeline-author`.

## Prefer declarative when the repo already does

```groovy
pipeline {
  agent any
  options {
    timestamps()
    timeout(time: 60, unit: 'MINUTES')
  }
  stages {
    stage('Check') {
      steps {
        // match existing checkout / test patterns in this repo
      }
    }
  }
  post {
    always {
      cleanWs()
    }
  }
}
```

- Keep stage names stable when dashboards or downstream jobs key on them.
- Prefer existing shared libraries / credentials bindings already used in
  neighboring Jenkinsfiles — do not invent a new `@Library` unless asked.
- Prefer `agent { label '…' }` only with labels the controller already
  exposes; do not invent agent labels.
- Use `options { timestamps(); timeout(…) }` when the repo pattern supports
  them; avoid unbounded stages on shared agents.
- Parallel stages: keep fan-out explicit and fail-fast behavior intentional
  (document if the project expects all branches to run).

## Scripted Pipeline

If the repo already uses scripted Pipeline (`node { … }`), stay scripted.
Do not convert declarative ↔ scripted in the same change unless the user
asked for a migration. Prefer the smallest edit that lands the stage or
fix.

## YAML / Jenkins Casc entrypoints

When the entrypoint is YAML (pipeline-as-code sidecars, folder config
snippets checked into git):

- 2-space indent
- Quote ambiguous scalars (`yes`/`no`/`on`/`off`, versions, digests)
- No secret values in the file — credential **ids** only
- One logical document per `---` section
- Do not invent controller Casc keys the repo does not already use

## Agnostic content

- No inline passwords, tokens, or private keys — use Jenkins credentials
  ids the job already references, or host-approved secret stores
- Do not hard-code machine-specific absolute paths unless the repo standard
  already does
- Parameterized jobs: declare `parameters { … }` explicitly; document
  defaults in the Jenkinsfile when the project expects that
- Environment: prefer credentials bindings / withCredentials over plain
  `environment { TOKEN = '…' }`
- Tools / JDK / Node: reuse `tools { }` or agent images already in the
  tree — do not pin a new global tool name without evidence it exists

## Shared libraries and trust

- Load only libraries the controller already trusts (`@Library` versions
  the org pins)
- Avoid `checkout scm` replacements that widen trust (extra remotes,
  force-push refs) without being asked
- Do not disable `sandbox` or add `MethodPermission` / script approvals
  unless the user explicitly requires it and peer pipelines already do

## Safety

- Destructive `post` / cleanup must not wipe unrelated workspaces or
  sibling job workspaces
- Do not add `input` gates that block shared executors indefinitely without
  timeout
- Archive/stash only what the pipeline needs; avoid archiving secrets or
  huge binaries by accident
- Multibranch: keep `when { branch … }` / change-request conditions aligned
  with the repo’s merge policy

## Verify

After editing, confirm with a build that checks out the new commit
(`jenkins-builds`). Do not claim the pipeline is fixed until that evidence
exists. Remind the operator that working-tree-only edits are invisible to
SCM-driven jobs (`jenkins-ci-from-main`).
